# Line endings.
#
# Why this file exists: a tool that rewrites a source file whole -- an editor, a
# formatter, an AI edit -- can silently flip its line endings, and git then
# records every line as changed. app/main.py went that way once: a 209-line
# change arrived as a 1,396-line diff, which is the kind of noise a real bug
# hides in. `text=auto eol=lf` makes git store LF no matter what landed on disk,
# so the diff stays the size of the actual edit.
*                                              text=auto eol=lf

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Legacy CRLF files, frozen as-is (`-text` = git never converts them).
#
# These came over from Motus with CRLF and are still committed that way. They
# WOULD renormalise to LF under the rule above -- which is the right end state,
# but it rewrites all twelve in one go, and doing that on a branch carrying
# in-flight work buries that work under whole-file diffs. So they are pinned to
# what they already are: no churn now, and no accidental conversion either.
#
# TODO: drop this whole block and run `git add --renormalize .` as its own
# commit, on a clean tree, after this branch merges. Nothing else needs to
# change -- the files are byte-identical apart from the CRs.
backend/app/static/index.html                  -text
backend/app/services/video_analysis/photo_analyzer.py       -text
backend/app/services/video_analysis/video_visualizer.py     -text
backend/app/services/video_analysis/biomechanics/*.py       -text
backend/app/services/video_analysis/biomechanics/reference_data/bike_reference.json -text

